<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lee Stack</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Lee Stack</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Lee Stack was a British army officer who served as Governor-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and Sirdar of the Egyptian Army from 1917. He was shot by Egyptian nationalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in November 1924 and died the next day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> responded with an ultimatum to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the expulsion of Egyptian troops and officials from the Sudan, strengthening British control of the condominium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lee-stack.jpg" alt="Lee Stack" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wide World Photos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Major_General_Sir_Lee_Stack_1924.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lee Oliver Fitzmaurice Stack was born in Darjeeling, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on 15 May 1868, the son of the British Inspector-General of Police for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. He was educated at Clifton College and at the Royal Military College, Sandhurst, and then served in the British Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Stack was seconded to the Egyptian Army in 1899 as a major. Besides regimental appointments he served as Military Secretary to General Sir Reginald Wingate, and in 1902 he received the Order of Osmanieh, third class, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt">Khedive of Egypt</a>. He left the army in 1910 but returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in 1913 as Civil Secretary, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>. At the outbreak of war in 1914 he was given the temporary rank of lieutenant-colonel, and in 1917 that of major-general on becoming Sirdar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 19 November 1924 Stack was being driven with an aide-de-camp from the Egyptian War Office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> to his official residence. When his car halted in heavy traffic to let a tram pass, several Egyptian students on the pavement fired a volley of revolver shots into it. His driver, Frederick Hamilton March, though wounded, drove on to the nearby residence of the British High Commissioner. Stack had been hit in the hand, stomach and foot.</p><p>The High Commissioner, Field Marshal Lord Allenby, demanded from the Egyptian government a public apology, an inquiry, the suppression of demonstrations and a fine, as well as the withdrawal of all Egyptian officers and units from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, a larger irrigation scheme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gezira">Gezira</a> and laws protecting foreign investors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>Seven men convicted of the killing were hanged in 1925. Several were identified by a taxi driver whose car they had seized to escape, and the pistols by an early use of bullet examination by the forensic scientist Sydney Smith. Sir Geoffrey Archer, formerly Governor of Uganda, succeeded Stack as Governor-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in January 1925, the first time a civilian had held the office.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lee Stack” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the assassination of governor-general <b>Lee Stack</b> in 1924, Egyptian troops were expelled.</blockquote><a href="/state/anglo-egyptian-sudan">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Darjeeling, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.04,88.26&amp;year=1904" aria-label="Darjeeling on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lee Stack"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4868 5252h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4868 5252h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.04,88.26&amp;year=1904">What was here in 1904? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
